\section{GEE Model Diagnostics}
\label{app:gee_diagnostics}

\paragraph{Correlation structure robustness.}
Table~\ref{tab:gee_corr} compares exchangeable and independence working correlation structures across all six judge$\times$axis cells.
The two structures yield virtually identical results: $|\Delta\text{QIC}| < 0.001$ and maximum coefficient change ${<}0.02$ in every cell, with estimated within-pair correlations $\hat{\alpha} \in [-0.25, +0.11]$.
All GEE results reported in the main text are therefore robust to correlation structure specification.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{GEE correlation structure comparison across six judge$\times$axis cells.}
\label{tab:gee_corr}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Config & Exch.\ QIC & Indep.\ QIC & $|\Delta\text{QIC}|$ & Max$|\Delta\text{Coef}|$ \\
\midrule
GPT-4o form.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
Qwen3 form.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
GPT-4o verb.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
Qwen3 verb.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
GPT-4o reg.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
Qwen3 reg.\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Interaction test.}
To verify that the additive GEE specification is appropriate, we test a style$\times$originality interaction term on formality data for both judges.
The interaction is non-significant in both cases (GPT-4o: $p = 0.53$; Qwen3-32B: $p = 0.32$), confirming that the additive model is the correct specification and that style and originality effects operate independently.

